---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Checker from '../components/checker/Checker';
import RebateBands from '../components/RebateBands.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../lib/rebateRules';
---

<Base
  title="New Home GST/HST Rebate Checker — Ontario Pre-Construction | The Precon Market"
  description="Find out in two minutes if you qualify for up to $130,000 in new home GST/HST rebates on your Ontario pre-construction purchase. Built by a licensed broker."
>
  <!-- Title block. Reads as drawing metadata, doubles as a freshness marker. -->
  <div class="border-b border-rule bg-paper-pure/60">
    <div
      class="mx-auto flex max-w-[1100px] flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-1 px-4 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] tracking-[0.08em] text-rule-dark uppercase sm:px-6"
    >
      <span>Ontario</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="hidden sm:inline text-rule">/</span>
      <span>Pre-construction</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="hidden sm:inline text-rule">/</span>
      <span>GST-HST relief</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="hidden sm:inline text-rule">/</span>
      <span>Rev {LAST_UPDATED}</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-[1100px] px-4 sm:px-6">
    <!-- DOM order is the mobile order: headline, then the tool. Desktop
         re-places these into two columns so the checker is never pushed
         below the fold on a phone. -->
    <div class="grid gap-8 pt-8 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-x-12 lg:gap-y-8 lg:pt-14">
      <div class="lg:col-span-5 lg:col-start-1 lg:row-start-1">
        <p class="section-label">— 01 / Eligibility check</p>
        <h1 class="mt-4 text-[1.95rem] leading-[1.1] font-semibold sm:text-[2.5rem]">
          Find out if you qualify for up to{' '}
          <span class="figure whitespace-nowrap text-accent">$130,000</span>{' '}
          in new home tax rebates.
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[46ch] text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink/75">
          Two new rebate programs took effect this year. Most buyers are still working from last year's numbers, and
          on a typical GTA condo the difference runs into six figures.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="lg:col-span-7 lg:col-start-6 lg:row-span-2 lg:row-start-1">
        <Checker client:load />
        <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-ink/45">
          Rebate rules current as of {LAST_UPDATED}. Programs are time-limited and subject to change.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Credential block, set like a drawing stamp. -->
      <div class="lg:col-span-5 lg:col-start-1 lg:row-start-2 lg:self-start">
        <div class="tick-corners border border-rule bg-paper-pure/70 p-4">
          <p class="section-label">— Prepared by</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm font-medium text-ink">Jordan Buttarazzi, Broker</p>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink/70">Valery Real Estate Inc., Brokerage</p>
          <p class="figure mt-2 text-[11px] text-ink/50">RECO #5009855 · (647) 371-1367</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Where the number comes from, drawn rather than listed. -->
    <section aria-labelledby="bands-heading" class="mt-20 border-t border-rule-dark pt-8">
      <p class="section-label">— 02 / Relief by price</p>
      <h2 id="bands-heading" class="mt-3 max-w-[28ch] text-2xl font-semibold">
        What you can actually claim, and where it stops.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink/80">
        Relief comes from two directions at once, and they behave differently as price climbs. The federal rebate is
        the one everybody talks about, but it's restricted to first-time buyers and it's gone by $1.5 million. The
        Ontario enhanced rebate is the bigger of the two, and it is <span class="text-ink">not</span> limited to
        first-time buyers. That surprises almost everyone, including plenty of agents.
      </p>

      <RebateBands />

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-[68ch] border-l-2 border-accent pl-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink/80">
        The two don't simply add to $180,000. Ontario's 5% top-up is reduced by whatever federal rebate you claim,
        which is what pins realistic combined relief around <span class="figure">$130,000</span>. Where you land in
        these bands depends on your agreement date, not your closing date, and the windows are narrow.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Depth signal, written as prose rather than another checklist. -->
    <section aria-labelledby="reads-heading" class="mt-20 border-t border-rule pt-8 pb-4">
      <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-12">
        <div class="lg:col-span-4">
          <p class="section-label">— 03 / Beyond the number</p>
          <h2 id="reads-heading" class="mt-3 text-2xl font-semibold">The estimate is the easy part.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink/80 lg:col-span-8">
          <p>
            Almost every builder agreement prices the unit "inclusive of HST" and assigns the rebate to the builder at
            closing. That's normal. What buyers miss is the consequence: CRA holds <span class="text-ink">you</span>
            {' '}responsible for the eligibility, and if it fails, you repay the rebate the builder already took credit
            for. A rebate credited on your statement of adjustments is not a rebate you've kept.
          </p>
          <p>
            The traps cluster in predictable places. A buyer who has never owned anything loses first-time status
            because they lived in a home their partner owned. An agreement amended in April 2026 gets deemed back to
            its original date and falls out of the enhanced window. A unit bought to rent moves onto an entirely
            different path where the HST is paid in full at closing and clawed back from CRA months later, which is a
            cash problem long before it's a tax problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            The checker flags which of these apply to you. Reading the actual clause in your actual agreement is the
            part that needs a person, and that's the part I do.
          </p>
          <p class="pt-2">
            <a
              href="/book?source=home-depth"
              class="inline-flex min-h-12 items-center border border-accent px-6 font-medium text-accent hover:bg-accent-tint"
            >
              Have your agreement reviewed
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
